{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"0309cf158f037c62c7999bc4e328a21a5ec0d0ba0d34c06c6cf9b555e1d59a80","slug":"stability-review","location":"/weather_technology/skewt/stability_review.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Atmospheric Stability","content_includes_title":false,"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","note":"All non-code content is licensed under CC-BY-4.0","free":true,"CC":true},"code":{"id":"Apache-2.0","url":"https://opensource.org/licenses/Apache-2.0","name":"Apache License 2.0","note":"All code content is licensed under Apache-2.0","free":true,"osi":true}},"github":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook","edit_url":null,"numbering":{"title":{"enabled":false,"offset":2},"equation":{"enabled":false},"figure":{"enabled":true,"continue":true}},"keywords":["metpy","python","meteorology","weather"],"copyright":"2026","settings":{"output_stderr":"remove-warn","output_matplotlib_strings":"remove"},"source_url":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook/blob/main/weather_technology/skewt/stability_review.md","thumbnail":"/build/stability-d6e7d6fa2cd2a4a1edd5692fccdb76fc.jpg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"stability_review.md","url":"/build/stability_review-da0b113aba074486da4784a39a0a3110.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The stability of the atmosphere can be described using the three\ndifferent conditions of equilibrium: stable, neutral, or unstable (Fig.\n1). When a ball is displaced up either side of a valley, gravity will\nwork to bring the ball back to its starting point. This would be\ndescribed as a stable system. If the ball was on top of a ridge, any\ndisplacement would cause the ball would move quickly away from its\ninitial point - this is defined as an unstable system. Finally, when a\nball is displaced and does not act to return to its original starting\nposition or move farther away from the original starting point, the\nstability would be defined as neutral.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"uBcrmjI19k"}],"key":"xS3k7ChAMd"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/build/stability-d6e7d6fa2cd2a4a1edd5692fccdb76fc.jpg","alt":"Stability types - stable, unstable, neutral","width":"300px","align":"center","key":"v0TTgQyalV","urlSource":"../../images/stability.jpg"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"stability","identifier":"stability","html_id":"stability","enumerator":"35","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"rJewTCUhkQ"},{"type":"text","value":"35","key":"U64TLcSU7V"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"Tp83n4KZ69"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"HecBuqsSPV"},{"type":"text","value":"Different conditions of equilibrium related to stability.\nImage from the ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"sYXWAt9J5f"},{"type":"link","url":"https://serc.carleton.edu/introgeo/models/EqStBOT.html","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equilibrium, Stability, and Behavior over time","key":"MCokvDtXiL"}],"urlSource":"https://serc.carleton.edu/introgeo/models/EqStBOT.html","key":"kCUVXZKJd0"},{"type":"text","value":" and is licensed under the ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"tsXuLsVmA0"},{"type":"link","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/deed.en","children":[{"type":"text","value":"“Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0 International”","key":"dzTW305mcJ"}],"urlSource":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/deed.en","key":"bUOb7XQeP9"},{"type":"text","value":". Image source ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"JV9YP40xH4"},{"type":"link","url":"https://serc.carleton.edu/images/introgeo/models/Stability.jpg","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"https://​serc​.carleton​.edu​/images​/introgeo​/models​/Stability​.jpg","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"Gy3Rt0S2Ra"}],"urlSource":"https://serc.carleton.edu/images/introgeo/models/Stability.jpg","key":"rcNGYwVBlj"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"ifxy3Vzqe3"}],"key":"qmJIvxeLDw"}],"key":"flpMyulCAg"}],"label":"stability","identifier":"stability","enumerator":"35","html_id":"stability","key":"wN8WwXhXcv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"These conditions can also be applied to the atmosphere. For parcels\ninitially displaced upwards, those wanting to rise without any forcing\nwould be ","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"fUa7bvk5hM"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"unstable","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"dQezTuctut"}],"key":"z1ti5pB4BE"},{"type":"text","value":"; those wanting to sink would be ","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"S3NVCpg0nX"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"stable","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"RZiLef4cpr"}],"key":"SkQdUiOFdn"},{"type":"text","value":"; those that\nwould need more forcing in order to rise freely would be ","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"RY0xdAXNCp"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"conditionally\nunstable","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"FeikHoY5YT"}],"key":"UHxxN1t1ch"},{"type":"text","value":"; all others would be ","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"Nwwgv5l5U9"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"neutral","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"GuolemvslH"}],"key":"W51QpFwaAa"},{"type":"text","value":". We will now apply these\nequilibrium types to the atmosphere and use parcel theory to determine\nthe stability of the atmosphere.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"tSbu1TN5PC"}],"key":"CxGSU5CcRg"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parcel Theory","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"LZrqpjrOfz"}],"identifier":"parcel-theory","label":"Parcel Theory","html_id":"parcel-theory","implicit":true,"key":"ijvnG5DVc2"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In meteorology, parcel theory is used to determine and describe the\nstability of the Earth-atmosphere system. A parcel is a fictitious blob\nof air that has similar moisture and temperature characteristics\nthroughout the parcel. Parcel sizes vary from the size of a large\nplayground ball to as big as a parking lot. To envision what parcels may\nlook like you could think of the bubbles in boiling water or the blobs\nof wax that move up and down in lava lamps. The mechanisms in the\natmosphere are similar to both of those examples; a parcel of water or\nwax is heated from below, just as the sun heats the atmosphere from\nbelow.","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"WdyDO1pnL3"}],"key":"zZ9gXi8VJq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether the atmosphere is stable, unstable, or conditionally unstable,\nrising motion can occur and lead to the development of different types\nof clouds. The development of clouds is predicated on the fact that\nthere is sufficient moisture to go along with the rising motion. In\nstable layers with sufficient moisture and rising motion, sheet like\nclouds will form (e.g., stratus, altostratus). In unstable or\nconditionally unstable portions of the atmosphere with sufficient\nmoisture puffy clouds (e.g., cumulus, cumulonimbus) often form.","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"iooA1AcLnh"}],"key":"auClTO8kah"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When air parcels rise, they always, and for all time will, ","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"BOEB7iCuER"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"cool","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"sKoljag49l"}],"key":"kQ1Zycl4mq"},{"type":"text","value":".\nDepending on the exact atmospheric conditions, the parcels will cool at\none of two lapse rates. If the air is not saturated (< 100% RH;\nunsaturated) then rising parcels will cool at the Dry Adiabatic Lapse\nRate (DALR) of 9.8 ","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"QttCRSgoqM"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"K","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">K</span></span></span></span>","key":"MlDb5JMWEv"},{"type":"text","value":" ","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"Bp3uhTHMFh"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"km^{-1}","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">km^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"RAStkeer5f"},{"type":"text","value":". For parcels with 100% RH, if they continue\nto rise, will cool at the Wet Adiabatic Lapse Rate (WALR), also known as\nthe Moist Adiabatic Lapse Rate (MALR). The WALR is not constant and\nvaries greatly over the temperature and pressure profile; consult a\nskew-T/log-p diagram to confirm this fact.","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"VAF5bJB8I4"}],"key":"ZUdDdCih7k"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"WALR vs. DALR","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"Hlr7xsDhp1"}],"identifier":"walr-vs-dalr","label":"WALR vs. DALR","html_id":"walr-vs-dalr","implicit":true,"key":"fYwt9Oblf0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The WALR is actually not an adiabatic process. Adiabatic motions are\nsuch that there is no net gain or loss of energy; the change in\ntemperature comes through the expansion (cooling) or compression\n(warming) of parcels. If you continue to cool air that is already\nsaturated, according to the Clausius-Clayperon equation, less water\nvapor can be present in cooler air, so it must be converted from water\nvapor to liquid water or ice. This is one way in which clouds are\nformed. So air parcels are still rising and cooling at the DALR, but\nwhen the water vapor is converted to liquid water, latent energy is\nreleased to the air parcel, which will slightly warm the parcel. This\nresults in a slightly lower cooling rate for a saturated parcel.","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":64,"column":1}},"key":"MTsyobCnlo"}],"key":"K5CtoLv2A5"}],"key":"VHOxmURUEz"}],"key":"pDuNeMkwS7"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Skew-T Log-p Diagram","url":"/skewt-intro","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"},"next":{"title":"Skew-T Log-p Description","url":"/skewt-diagram","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3001"}